1
00:00:00,940 --> 00:00:03,760
Now that you've understood some of the basic principles

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,360
of normalcytic anemias, we're now going to apply

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:09,560
those principles to discuss some of the specific diseases

4
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:10,480
in these categories.

5
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:13,580
The way I've divided this in your text is to talk about

6
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:16,920
normalcytic anemias with predominant extravascular hemolysis,

7
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:19,160
and then we'll talk about normalcytic anemias

8
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:21,960
with predominant intravascular hemolysis,

9
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:24,800
and then we'll finally talk about underproduction anemias.

10
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:27,620
It's important to note here that I used the word

11
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:31,520
predominant to remind you that these are not absolute.

12
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:33,980
In some diseases in which there is mostly

13
00:00:33,980 --> 00:00:36,680
extravascular hemolysis, there will be some degree

14
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:40,360
of intravascular hemolysis often, and so we don't want

15
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:42,320
to be absolute in the way we think about these,

16
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:45,000
and I'll hope to point that out to you as we move forward

17
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,120
because examiners also like to play on this notion

18
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,380
that these divisions are not absolute.

19
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:53,820
I'll highlight that a little later in our discussion as well.

20
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:56,760
So the first of the disorders that we're going to discuss

21
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:59,900
is hereditary spherocytosis, and perhaps it's easiest

22
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:03,480
if I just explain to you via an image before we actually

23
00:01:03,480 --> 00:01:04,080
move forward.

24
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:09,120
Recall that the red blood cell has a shape which is specialized,

25
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:13,200
which gives it a biconcave disc, and this red blood cell,

26
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:14,940
in order to be able to maintain the shape,

27
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,840
has a cytoskeleton that assists in maintaining the shape.

28
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,480
The cytoskeleton is tethered to the cell membrane,

29
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:25,140
which then allows for the cytoskeleton to perform the function

30
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:26,200
of maintaining the shape.

31
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:30,540
In hereditary spherocytosis, the problem is that the tethering

32
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:32,960
molecules between the cytoskeleton and the cell membrane

33
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:33,720
are disrupted.

34
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:35,720
It's an inherited defect, actually.

35
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:39,380
And this results in small little blebs of membrane coming off

36
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:40,780
of the red blood cell.

37
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:44,540
These little blebs are then removed by the spleen when the red blood

38
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:47,380
cell moves through the spleen.

39
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:51,500
And over time, as you begin to remove membrane, you lose the ability

40
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:54,660
to push the membrane in here and push the membrane in here.

41
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:58,620
And so instead, what happens is the cell becomes spherocytic like this.

42
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:00,380
And that's hereditary spherocytosis.

43
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:03,240
So with that in mind, let's go back and read the slide.

44
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,700
Hereditary spherocytosis is an inherited defect of the red blood

45
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:11,220
cell cytoskeletal membrane tethering proteins.

46
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:15,700
And the most common examples of these proteins that tend to be involved

47
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:20,520
in this disease include spectrin, ankerin, and band 3.1.

48
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:24,040
These are common tethering proteins that allow connection

49
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,140
between the cytoskeleton and the membrane.

50
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:31,920
Now, because of this, membrane blebs are lost over time,

51
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:33,040
as I've already illustrated.

52
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:37,520
The loss of membranes renders the cells round, i.e. spherocytes,

53
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:38,920
instead of disc-shaped.

54
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:40,920
This is what the cells look like.

55
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:44,260
Notice that there is a loss of the central pallor.

56
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:48,100
And the loss of central pallor is due to the fact that we no longer

57
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:51,880
have the biconcave shape to the red blood cell.

58
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:56,180
At the same time, notice that there is a range in the size of the cells.

59
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:59,220
And the range in the size of the cells is due to the fact that,

60
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:03,800
remember I told you that when you have this red blood cell, like this,

61
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:07,100
you're losing a little bit of membrane, and then lose a little bit more membrane,

62
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:09,200
and then lose more membrane, and more membrane.

63
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:13,480
And so over time, what's happening is that cells keep losing membrane.

64
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:16,780
So the oldest cells will have lost the most membrane,

65
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:19,320
and the youngest cells will have lost the least membrane.

66
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:22,100
But this is going to create a high RDW,

67
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:24,420
a high red blood cell distribution width,

68
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:28,080
i.e. there will be some smaller cells and some larger cells.

69
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:29,880
And I don't think that's hard to remember

70
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:33,120
if you understand the basic pathology behind this disease.

71
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:37,640
In the end, once you get spherocytes that are very small,

72
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:40,260
they'll be less able to maneuver through the spleen.

73
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,100
And when they're less able to maneuver through the spleen,

74
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:46,200
they'll be consumed by the spleen, which will then cause the anemia.

75
00:03:46,780 --> 00:03:51,120
Otherwise stated, there really is no problem with having spherocytes.

76
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:54,540
The real problem is the fact that eventually the spherocytes

77
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:57,840
will become less able to maneuver through the spleen.

78
00:03:58,080 --> 00:03:59,900
And again, so the spleen will consume them.

79
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,900
Some of the clinical and laboratory features.

80
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:06,540
They include spherocytes with a loss of central pallor.

81
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:07,700
I've already shown you that.

82
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:09,380
An increased RDW.

83
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:10,580
I've already explained that.

84
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:12,800
And an increased MCHC.

85
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:14,080
What's MCHC?

86
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:16,920
The mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration.

87
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:19,780
As you begin to shrink down the cell,

88
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:22,240
the cytoplasm remains about the same.

89
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:26,060
And so the hemoglobin gets more concentrated within that cell.

90
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:29,900
And that is measured by the mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration.

91
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:32,220
It really is probably, for your purposes,

92
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:37,400
the only disease where you're actually going to see an increased MCHC.

93
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:40,980
The only other example I can think of would be autoimmune anemia.

94
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:43,520
But in autoimmune anemia, as I'm going to show you later,

95
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:46,200
you have a very similar process going on.

96
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:49,500
And again, you also get the production of spherocytes.

97
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:51,780
But we'll talk about that in a few minutes.

98
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:54,800
In addition, patients are going to have splenomegaly.

99
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:55,960
Why do they have splenomegaly?

100
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:59,480
Because the spleen is where the consumption of the spherocytes is occurring.

101
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:02,400
It's important to note, and examiners like to ask you,

102
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:05,000
that this is a work hypertrophy.

103
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:08,960
So that the macrophages of the spleen will undergo hypertrophy

104
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:12,360
as they begin to eat away at these red blood cells.

105
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:17,700
Patients will also develop jaundice with an unconjugated bilirubin.

106
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:21,480
And there will be an increased risk for bilirubin gallstones.

107
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:23,400
If you recall from my previous lecture,

108
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:25,480
the jaundice is very easy to understand.

109
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:28,400
As the spleen is eating away at the red blood cells,

110
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:32,960
it will eventually have to put out unconjugated bilirubin.

111
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:37,480
That unconjugated bilirubin will overwhelm the liver's ability to conjugate,

112
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,580
and so increased unconjugated bilirubin will be floating around in the blood,

113
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:42,860
hence the jaundice.

114
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:47,180
Over time, you're going to conjugate all of that bilirubin by the liver,

115
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:49,420
and the liver will throw it into the gallbladder,

116
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:53,420
and that's going to result in a very high concentration of bilirubin in the gallbladder,

117
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:56,080
increasing the risk for bilirubin gallstones.

118
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:00,420
These patients will also have an increased risk for aplastic crisis

119
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:02,440
with parvovirus B19 infection.

120
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:04,420
We've already covered this, but again,

121
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:07,280
patients who have limited reserve in their bone marrow

122
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:10,220
are very susceptible to becoming symptomatic

123
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:13,280
were they to get a parvovirus B19 infection.

124
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:15,500
And again, high yield for examinations.

125
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:19,160
Parvovirus B19 infects the erythroid precursors.

126
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:24,440
The diagnosis of hereditary spherocytosis occurs by the osmotic fragility test.

127
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:27,940
And the idea here is that if you have a red blood cell

128
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:31,360
that has its normal biconcave shape to it,

129
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:35,900
and you introduce this red blood cell into a hypotonic solution,

130
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:39,080
water will come into the cell.

131
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:41,200
And when water comes into the cell,

132
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:44,400
there is some room for this cell to expand here and here

133
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:46,800
because there's an extra membrane pushed in here

134
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:50,320
that can actually expand out both ways before the cell will burst.

135
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:55,360
So the normal cell is relatively resistant to a hypotonic solution.

136
00:06:55,840 --> 00:06:58,480
However, if you actually had a spherocyte,

137
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:02,480
the spherocyte has no extra membrane because the membrane's been removed.

138
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:05,580
And so were this cell to be placed in a hypotonic solution,

139
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:09,480
water would come in and that would cause the cell to burst very easily.

140
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:13,960
So this cell is fragile in a hypotonic solution.

141
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:16,280
And that's the osmotic fragility test.

142
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:19,760
There is an increased fragility in hypotonic solution.

143
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:23,180
The treatment for this disease is splenectomy, and that makes sense.

144
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:23,600
Why?

145
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:26,240
Because really the problem was in the spleen.

146
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:28,920
The spleen was where the red blood cells were being eaten.

147
00:07:29,340 --> 00:07:31,480
I told you there's no problem with having spherocytes.

148
00:07:31,580 --> 00:07:33,760
The problem is that the spleen eats the spherocytes,

149
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:35,080
which then creates the anemia.

150
00:07:35,560 --> 00:07:38,340
So if we treat with splenectomy, the anemia will go away.

151
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:40,700
The spherocytes will persist.

152
00:07:40,820 --> 00:07:42,100
Why will the spherocytes persist?

153
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:46,960
Because the cells continue to have a problem with membrane blubbing,

154
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:51,960
and the cells will still go through some areas of the reticuloendothelial system,

155
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:54,140
for example, in the liver and in the lymph nodes.

156
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:58,300
And so some membrane will be removed by the reticuloendothelial system.

157
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,340
However, the anemia will resolve.

158
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:07,560
And then finally, the other finding is that we'll begin to see howl-jolly bodies on the blood smear.

159
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:10,080
This requires a little bit of explanation.

160
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:15,140
Remember that when a red blood cell is born, the red blood cell's nucleus is removed,

161
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:18,940
and the vast majority of time the nucleus is completely removed.

162
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:24,200
However, on occasion, there can be a red blood cell in which there is a fragment of nuclear material

163
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:25,480
that did not get removed.

164
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:32,120
It is the job of the spleen to go in and to remove that extra fragment of nuclear material.

165
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:35,360
Basically, if the spleen sees anything abnormal with the red blood cell,

166
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:38,900
it tends to remove it or to remove the red blood cell completely.

167
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:45,020
So normally, the spleen would remove little fragments of DNA that might be present in a rare cell or so.

168
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:49,560
However, in the case of hereditary spherocytosis,

169
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:53,440
if the patient has undergone splenectomy, the spleen will still not be present.

170
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:58,280
And therefore, these little fragments of DNA that normally would be removed by the spleen

171
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:00,520
will remain in the red blood cell.

172
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:05,300
And that's called a Howell-jolly body, and this is a classic image of a Howell-jolly body.

173
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:07,760
Again, it indicates splenic dysfunction.

174
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:14,720
The next of the normalcytic anemias in which there is predominantly extravascular hemolysis

175
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:16,020
is sickle cell anemia.

176
00:09:16,780 --> 00:09:20,540
In sickle cell anemia, the patients have an autosomal recessive mutation

177
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:25,760
in the beta chain of hemoglobin such that the normal glutamic acid,

178
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:29,940
which is hydrophilic, gets replaced with valine, which is hydrophobic.

179
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:34,260
So it's a single amino acid chain that results in this disease,

180
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:36,380
and that's called sickle cell anemia.

181
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:41,620
The gene for this disease is carried by 10% of people of African descent,

182
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:45,120
and that's because it likely protects against falciparum malaria.

183
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:49,920
And this is a theme that we've already covered as we've gone through this particular chapter.

184
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:54,780
When patients have two abnormally mutated beta chains, that's called sickle cell disease,

185
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:58,140
and it results in greater than 90% production of hemoglobin S.

186
00:09:58,460 --> 00:09:59,240
Here's the idea.

187
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:01,100
We know that we have two beta genes.

188
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:04,260
So here's beta gene 1, and here's beta gene 2.

189
00:10:04,660 --> 00:10:08,220
Imagine if this is beta gene that is the sickle mutation,

190
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:11,120
and this beta gene also has a sickle mutation,

191
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:14,860
then when this beta gene produces beta, you'll get beta 2 dimers,

192
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:18,320
and when this beta gene produces beta, we'll get beta 2 dimers,

193
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:22,880
and of course, this will dimerize with alpha 2, and also with alpha 2.

194
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:25,320
But in both cases, this is beta S.

195
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:28,620
So when you have alpha 2, beta 2, and it's a beta S,

196
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:34,840
this equals hemoglobin S, and this also equals hemoglobin S.

197
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:41,640
So the primary hemoglobin that's going to be produced in these patients

198
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:43,920
is going to be hemoglobin S,

199
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:48,880
and that's why we say it results in greater than 90% hemoglobin S in the red blood cells.

200
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:51,180
Now what's the problem with hemoglobin S?

201
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:54,120
It preliminers when it's deoxygenated,

202
00:10:54,640 --> 00:10:56,260
and that's important to understand,

203
00:10:56,460 --> 00:11:00,840
that when these cells are deoxygenated or placed under certain stresses,

204
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:04,840
for example, hypoxemia, dehydration, acidosis,

205
00:11:05,140 --> 00:11:09,080
all of these circumstances will result in polymerization of the hemoglobin,

206
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:15,980
which then is going to allow the hemoglobin molecules to aggregate into needle-like structures,

207
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:20,220
which is then going to cause the cells to take on a banana shape or a sickle shape.

208
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:24,420
Important to note for examinations that this is a polymerization.

209
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:26,500
It is not a covalent bonding.

210
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:30,400
It's a polymerization, and it is reversible.

211
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:32,740
This is what the sickle cells look like.

212
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:34,480
They kind of take on this banana shape,

213
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:38,540
and again, this is due to the polymerization of the hemoglobin S.

214
00:11:40,220 --> 00:11:45,080
The circumstances in which we would see an increased risk of polymerization of the hemoglobin

215
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:46,560
would be in hypoxemia.

216
00:11:46,900 --> 00:11:48,860
So when the hemoglobin is deoxygenated,

217
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:50,980
there'll be an increased risk of sickling.

218
00:11:50,980 --> 00:11:54,020
In dehydration, there's an increased risk of sickling.

219
00:11:54,380 --> 00:11:57,340
And in acidosis, there's an increased risk of sickling.

220
00:11:57,660 --> 00:12:01,000
So these are the three driving factors for sickling.

221
00:12:01,860 --> 00:12:04,960
One of the protective factors against sickling is hemoglobin F.

222
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:08,080
If hemoglobin F is present, it protects against sickling.

223
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:11,180
This is important because there are two derivative principles

224
00:12:11,180 --> 00:12:13,180
that are derived from this general idea.

225
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:18,140
Number one, this helps to explain why patients with sickle cell anemia

226
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:20,800
don't present until about six months of birth.

227
00:12:21,180 --> 00:12:22,680
For the first few months after birth,

228
00:12:22,780 --> 00:12:26,720
the predominant hemoglobin remains hemoglobin F from the fetus.

229
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,960
And so that high hemoglobin F during the first few months of life

230
00:12:29,960 --> 00:12:31,480
actually protects against sickling.

231
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:34,960
And these patients classically will present at about six months of age.

232
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:40,000
Another important principle that we can derive from this idea of hemoglobin F

233
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,080
protecting against sickling helps to explain

234
00:12:43,080 --> 00:12:46,600
why treatment with hydroxyurea is beneficial.

235
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:50,780
Hydroxyurea actually increases the levels of hemoglobin F.

236
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:52,640
We don't know why, but that's what it does.

237
00:12:53,020 --> 00:12:55,240
And that also helps to protect against sickling.

238
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:59,560
Now when blood cells with hemoglobin S go through the circulation,

239
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:01,020
they will actually sickle.

240
00:13:01,180 --> 00:13:04,160
Every time when oxygen is removed, the cells sickle.

241
00:13:04,540 --> 00:13:06,700
And then they quickly desickle.

242
00:13:07,140 --> 00:13:10,340
And they sickle and desickle and sickle and desickle

243
00:13:10,340 --> 00:13:12,840
every time they run through the microcirculation.

244
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:17,940
And this then is going to result in one of the important complications of sickle cell anemia,

245
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:21,720
which is that there's going to be membrane damage to the red blood cell.

246
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:25,780
So imagine that if every time the cell sickles,

247
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,800
the sort of the crystals are pricking away at the membrane,

248
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:34,060
then over time that's going to result in membrane damage to the red blood cell.

249
00:13:34,060 --> 00:13:41,340
And one of the major principles of clinical findings in sickle cell anemia

250
00:13:41,340 --> 00:13:43,640
is actually related to membrane damage.

251
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:46,220
For example, because there's membrane damage,

252
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:49,900
the cells are going to become less flexible over time.

253
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:51,800
And as they become less flexible over time,

254
00:13:51,900 --> 00:13:54,100
the spleen will actually remove them from the circulation.

255
00:13:54,880 --> 00:13:59,160
And so that's going to result in extravascular hemolysis with an anemia.

256
00:13:59,620 --> 00:14:02,920
At the same time, all of the consequences of extravascular hemolysis,

257
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:06,560
jaundice, as I've already explained, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia,

258
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:09,660
and increased risk for bilirubin gallstones

259
00:14:09,660 --> 00:14:13,120
will all be a consequence of extravascular hemolysis.

260
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:17,420
At the same time, some of the cells that are present in the blood

261
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:20,640
will actually lyse because the red cell membrane is being damaged.

262
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:24,400
That minimal amount, it's not the predominant mechanism of anemia,

263
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:27,840
but there is a decent amount of intravascular hemolysis

264
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:30,900
enough to decrease the serum heptoglobin.

265
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:34,040
So remember that heptoglobin is a scavenger molecule,

266
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:36,360
that when there is free hemoglobin in the blood,

267
00:14:36,760 --> 00:14:40,020
the heptoglobin is the first molecule that tries to save that hemoglobin,

268
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:42,460
and so heptoglobin will actually decrease

269
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:45,840
because it will be used up as the intravascular hemolysis occurs.

270
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:49,060
At the same time, there will be target cells on the blood smear.

271
00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:51,140
Why are there target cells on the blood smear?

272
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:56,120
These cells, as the membrane gets damaged, they begin to dehydrate.

273
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:02,340
And as they begin to dehydrate, the cytoplasm is going to be slowly decreased within those cells.

274
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:06,040
So, as I've already explained previously, but I'll explain one more time,

275
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:10,900
if this is the biconcave red blood cell and you begin to pull away cytoplasm,

276
00:15:11,140 --> 00:15:12,520
there's going to be extra membrane.

277
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:14,340
There's going to be redundancy in the membrane.

278
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:16,000
It's like taking air out of a basketball.

279
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,200
And so, a little bleb can actually form here and here,

280
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:21,040
which would be full of hemoglobin,

281
00:15:21,940 --> 00:15:28,660
which would then produce a red color both at the edges and in the center of the red blood cell.

282
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,960
And again, that's a target cell, and I've explained that previously as well.

283
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:37,160
Now, another consequence of the both intra- and extravascular hemolysis

284
00:15:37,160 --> 00:15:39,760
is that there's going to be massive erythroid hyperplasia.

285
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:41,780
The bone marrow will undergo hyperplasia,

286
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:44,140
trying to produce as many red cells as possible.

287
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:48,340
This will result of expansion of hematopoiesis into the skull and facial bones.

288
00:15:48,700 --> 00:15:52,840
When it goes into the skull, you're going to get a classic crew cut appearance on x-ray.

289
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:55,160
And when it goes into the facial bones,

290
00:15:55,220 --> 00:16:00,500
it's going to create that classic chipmunk facies that we've talked about in beta-thal major.

291
00:16:01,340 --> 00:16:05,860
Similarly, the patients will have extramedullary hematopoiesis with hepatomegaly,

292
00:16:05,980 --> 00:16:10,240
and that's because once the bone marrow is completely full of hematopoiesis,

293
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:15,040
the next place that we could actually develop hematopoiesis would be extramedullary,

294
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:17,420
for example, in the spleen and in the liver.

295
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:20,980
However, these patients usually don't have a spleen, as I'm going to talk about later,

296
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:22,860
so it's predominantly in the liver.

297
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:26,980
There's also going to be a risk of aplastic crisis with parvovirus B19.

298
00:16:27,560 --> 00:16:29,040
Again, we've already talked about that,

299
00:16:29,140 --> 00:16:33,700
so these discussions now are becoming easier as you're more familiar with some of the themes

300
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:35,000
that apply to red blood cells.

301
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:40,840
Another consequence of sickling is that eventually you can develop irreversible sickling,

302
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:43,040
and so this is sort of the second major problem.

303
00:16:43,180 --> 00:16:44,840
One problem was red blood cell damage,

304
00:16:45,260 --> 00:16:49,100
which then resulted in the anemia and all of those complications I've already discussed.

305
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:51,960
A second major problem is irreversible sickling.

306
00:16:52,420 --> 00:16:55,400
The irreversible sickling leads to vasoocclusion,

307
00:16:55,820 --> 00:16:58,700
and vasoocclusion can then result in infarction of tissues.

308
00:16:59,280 --> 00:17:03,220
The first classic example of vasoocclusion would be dactylitis.

309
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:06,920
Dactylitis refers to swelling of the bones of the hands and the feet,

310
00:17:07,080 --> 00:17:09,180
usually due to vasoocclusive infarction.

311
00:17:09,580 --> 00:17:13,780
This is the common presenting sign in infants, a very common presenting sign.

312
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:18,960
Examiners like to show you pictures in which they have pictures of swollen fingers

313
00:17:18,960 --> 00:17:22,340
and hands of usually an African-American infant,

314
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:25,780
and then a classic vignette of sickle cell anemia,

315
00:17:26,120 --> 00:17:28,880
and then you'll have to recognize that the patient has sickle cell anemia

316
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:33,340
or have to identify some derivative principle about sickle cell anemia.

317
00:17:33,740 --> 00:17:36,160
So dactylitis is very high yield to be familiar with.

318
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:39,700
Again, classically seen in a six-month-old infant

319
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:43,440
as it is a common presenting sign of sickle cell anemia.

320
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:47,920
Additionally, because they get vasoocclusive crises in the spleen,

321
00:17:48,240 --> 00:17:51,540
eventually the spleen gets infarcted and disappears.

322
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:53,380
That's called autosplenectomy.

323
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:55,940
It results in a shrunken fibrotic spleen.

324
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:59,200
Loss of the spleen then results in a series of problems.

325
00:17:59,340 --> 00:18:01,880
Number one, if the spleen is no longer present,

326
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:06,940
the primary source of antibody production, which is the spleen, is lost,

327
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:10,860
and there's going to be an increased risk of infection with encapsulated organisms.

328
00:18:11,380 --> 00:18:13,740
For example, H. flu, strep pneumo,

329
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:17,380
and this is very important because it's the most common cause of death

330
00:18:17,380 --> 00:18:19,760
in children with sickle cell disease.

331
00:18:19,760 --> 00:18:26,140
So it's very important to vaccinate these kids against the bugs

332
00:18:26,140 --> 00:18:28,180
that they're most likely to be infected with.

333
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:31,460
That's one complication of autosplenectomy.

334
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:36,740
A second complication of autosplenectomy is an increased risk of salmonella osteomyelitis.

335
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:41,140
And whereas the most common cause of osteomyelitis overall is staph aureus,

336
00:18:41,480 --> 00:18:44,840
in patients with sickle cell, the most common cause is salmonella.

337
00:18:45,380 --> 00:18:49,580
And then the third consequence of autosplenectomy is how well jolly bodies on blood smear.

338
00:18:49,760 --> 00:18:51,180
I've already explained this to you.

339
00:18:51,260 --> 00:18:53,680
Red blood cells normally remove the entire nucleus.

340
00:18:54,180 --> 00:18:58,220
However, in some cases, the nucleus may not be completely removed,

341
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:03,060
and it's the job of the spleen to go in and remove that little extra bit of nuclear material.

342
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:05,600
If the spleen is not present or not functional,

343
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:10,900
that extra material will remain in the rare red blood cell where it actually was present,

344
00:19:11,320 --> 00:19:13,000
and that's called a how well jolly body.

345
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,520
And I've already explained this just a few moments ago as well.

346
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:22,160
Additionally, vaso-occlusive crises can occur within the vessels of the lung,

347
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:27,460
and that's called acute chest syndrome, vaso-occlusion in pulmonary microcirculation.

348
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:32,200
Classically presents with chest pain, shortness of breath, and infiltrates on the lung.

349
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:34,360
It's usually precipitated by pneumonia.

350
00:19:34,520 --> 00:19:36,920
Now, the idea here is that the patient gets a lung infection,

351
00:19:36,920 --> 00:19:41,880
and when they get a lung infection, the lung infection then causes vasodilation

352
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:45,520
and causes the blood flow to slow down a little bit,

353
00:19:45,900 --> 00:19:49,980
and the slowing down of the blood flow increases the transit time for the red blood cells,

354
00:19:50,340 --> 00:19:54,800
and an increased transit time can increase dehydration, can increase acidemia,

355
00:19:55,320 --> 00:20:01,040
can also increase the deoxygenation of the red blood cells,

356
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:02,720
which would then promote sickling.

357
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:04,520
So this is the classic finding.

358
00:20:04,620 --> 00:20:06,800
The patient gets a pneumonia, and then after the pneumonia,

359
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:08,540
they develop an acute chest syndrome.

360
00:20:09,020 --> 00:20:12,300
Important to note because this is the most common cause of death in adults.

361
00:20:12,740 --> 00:20:16,260
So in summary, what I would say is that the presenting sign is dactylitis.

362
00:20:16,700 --> 00:20:21,520
The most common cause of death in kids is going to be infection with encapsulated organisms,

363
00:20:21,760 --> 00:20:26,140
and the most common cause of death in adults is actually acute chest syndrome.

364
00:20:26,700 --> 00:20:29,200
Vaso-occlusion can also result in pain crises,

365
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:34,220
for example, in other bones, in other organs, in other tissues.

366
00:20:35,180 --> 00:20:39,600
You can get a vaso-occlusive crisis in the kidney,

367
00:20:40,140 --> 00:20:43,100
and that can result in renal papillary necrosis,

368
00:20:43,160 --> 00:20:47,960
and the classic finding in renal papillary necrosis is gross hematuria and proteinuria.

369
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:51,600
And so these are all complications of vaso-occlusive crises.

370
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:55,200
Now, of course, sickle cell is a very complicated disease,

371
00:20:55,320 --> 00:20:57,520
and we could discuss many, many more findings,

372
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:01,780
but this is sort of the take-home paradigm that you need to bury away in your mind.

373
00:21:03,300 --> 00:21:07,740
If a patient only has one mutated beta gene, then we call that sickle cell trait.

374
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:11,480
And you'll have to go back and remember that we've got two beta genes,

375
00:21:11,480 --> 00:21:18,270
and this is beta-s, and this is beta, the normal beta.

376
00:21:18,590 --> 00:21:21,270
So this patient has one mutated gene and one normal gene.

377
00:21:21,730 --> 00:21:26,110
Now, when they make this beta, they're going to make beta-2 tetramers that are normal,

378
00:21:26,510 --> 00:21:30,430
and when they make this beta, they're going to make beta-2 tetramers that are sickle,

379
00:21:30,730 --> 00:21:33,990
and, of course, alpha-2 will join here, and alpha-2 will join here,

380
00:21:34,310 --> 00:21:37,490
and so these patients will produce both hemoglobin A,

381
00:21:37,630 --> 00:21:39,670
this is hemoglobin A,

382
00:21:39,910 --> 00:21:42,550
and they'll also produce hemoglobin S.

383
00:21:42,550 --> 00:21:44,690
So now they're producing both hemoglobins.

384
00:21:45,190 --> 00:21:48,070
What's important to note here is that the production of beta

385
00:21:48,070 --> 00:21:51,750
is a little more efficient than the production of beta-S,

386
00:21:52,050 --> 00:21:56,930
and so they'll actually get, like, for example, 55% hemoglobin A

387
00:21:56,930 --> 00:21:59,910
and maybe 45% hemoglobin S.

388
00:22:00,370 --> 00:22:06,110
Again, what's important to note is that they're going to have less than 50% hemoglobin S.

389
00:22:06,670 --> 00:22:07,970
Why am I highlighting that?

390
00:22:07,970 --> 00:22:13,050
Because you need about 50% of hemoglobin S to actually sickle,

391
00:22:13,450 --> 00:22:17,350
and if the patients have less than 50% hemoglobin S, they won't sickle.

392
00:22:17,750 --> 00:22:22,950
So generally, these patients who have sickle cell trait are going to be asymptomatic.

393
00:22:23,770 --> 00:22:29,010
That's because red blood cells with less than 50% hemoglobin S don't usually sickle in vivo.

394
00:22:29,010 --> 00:22:32,210
There is one exception, however, and that's the renal medulla.

395
00:22:32,730 --> 00:22:36,070
In the renal medulla, there's extreme hypoxia and hypertonicity,

396
00:22:36,470 --> 00:22:39,510
and that extreme hypoxia and hypertonicity results in sickling.

397
00:22:39,930 --> 00:22:41,010
The patients get microinfarctions.

398
00:22:42,150 --> 00:22:44,750
Initially, it presents with microscopic hematuria.

399
00:22:45,190 --> 00:22:49,690
Eventually, the classic finding is a decreased ability to concentrate the urine.

400
00:22:50,150 --> 00:22:53,350
All of that is high yield concerning sickle cell trait.

401
00:22:54,210 --> 00:22:58,070
Generally speaking, whether we're talking about sickle cell anemia or sickle cell trait,

402
00:22:58,470 --> 00:23:00,770
it's important to note some basic laboratory findings.

403
00:23:01,550 --> 00:23:05,990
Sickle cells and target cells would be seen in sickle cell disease, but not in trait.

404
00:23:06,390 --> 00:23:09,210
And you know that, because in trait, the patients don't sickle,

405
00:23:09,290 --> 00:23:13,550
so we wouldn't normally see sickling red blood cells on a smear.

406
00:23:13,550 --> 00:23:19,750
However, were we to take metabisulfite, which is a chemical that can induce cells to sickle,

407
00:23:20,210 --> 00:23:25,930
then if there is any degree of hemoglobin S, whether it be 50% or less than 50%,

408
00:23:25,930 --> 00:23:27,990
it will cause the cell to sickle.

409
00:23:28,430 --> 00:23:34,590
So otherwise stated, a metabisulfite screen allows you to identify hemoglobin S in any cell.

410
00:23:35,090 --> 00:23:38,510
Therefore, it's positive in both disease and trait.

411
00:23:38,630 --> 00:23:42,550
So it's a nice screening test if you're worried about sickle cell trait, for example.

412
00:23:43,550 --> 00:23:47,610
Hemoglobin electrophoresis can also be useful to confirm the presence of hemoglobin S

413
00:23:47,610 --> 00:23:49,010
and exactly how much there is.

414
00:23:49,490 --> 00:23:54,250
So, for example, in disease, we know that the vast majority of hemoglobin will be hemoglobin S,

415
00:23:54,570 --> 00:23:56,730
and then there will be some F and some A2.

416
00:23:57,070 --> 00:24:00,030
But there will be no A, because there is no normal beta chain.

417
00:24:00,130 --> 00:24:05,370
Remember, hemoglobin A is really composed of alpha 2 and beta 2,

418
00:24:05,790 --> 00:24:08,170
and these patients don't have any normal beta 2.

419
00:24:08,710 --> 00:24:12,470
In trait, there will be hemoglobin A, because they do have one normal beta gene.

420
00:24:12,790 --> 00:24:14,490
And it will be about 55%.

421
00:24:14,490 --> 00:24:19,390
There will be hemoglobin S, which will be less than 50%, and then a little bit of A2.

422
00:24:19,870 --> 00:24:25,110
So this is important, again, but I've already highlighted some of this in our previous discussions.

423
00:24:25,110 --> 00:24:30,310
Another important hemoglobinopathy to be familiar with is hemoglobin C.

424
00:24:30,770 --> 00:24:35,710
Now, the problem here is that you have an autosomal recessive mutation, again, in the beta chain of hemoglobin,

425
00:24:36,090 --> 00:24:41,250
but the normal glutamic acid, instead of being replaced by valine, as we had in sickle cell disease,

426
00:24:41,630 --> 00:24:42,850
is replaced by lysine.

427
00:24:42,850 --> 00:24:47,450
So that's the first point, is that hemoglobin C has lysine.

428
00:24:47,730 --> 00:24:49,790
Hemoglobin C has lysine.

429
00:24:49,990 --> 00:24:50,930
Maybe that might help you.

430
00:24:51,870 --> 00:24:54,070
This is less common than sickle cell disease.

431
00:24:54,450 --> 00:24:57,270
It presents with a mild anemia.

432
00:24:58,130 --> 00:25:00,690
The anemia is predominantly due to extravascular hemolysis.

433
00:25:00,690 --> 00:25:05,710
But what's most important about this disease, especially for examination purposes,

434
00:25:05,710 --> 00:25:10,990
is that you characteristically see hemoglobin C crystals on the blood smear.

435
00:25:11,370 --> 00:25:14,090
And this is the classic hemoglobin C crystal.

436
00:25:14,530 --> 00:25:17,850
So lysine and hemoglobin C crystals.

437
00:25:18,390 --> 00:25:24,010
That concludes our discussion of normocytic anemias with mostly extravascular hemolysis.

438
00:25:24,010 --> 00:25:30,110
And our next discussion is going to be normocytic anemias with mostly extravascular hemolysis.

